/* =====================================================================================================
   MANUAL SQL UPGRADE SCRIPT
   Migration: 202608191533567_DeprecateTeamsAddons
   =====================================================================================================
   For operators / DBAs who upgrade the Analytics database schema BY HAND instead of running the installer
   (which applies EF migrations automatically). It performs the same schema changes as the migration and
   then stamps __MigrationHistory so EF (DatabaseUpgrader / MigrateDatabaseToLatestVersion) and the web
   app Health page recognise it as applied.

   BACKGROUND
     Teams add-on / app-install tracking is DEPRECATED. The import that populated these tables has been
     removed from the product entirely, along with its EF entities and the "Teams app installs" category
     on the user-data lookup page. The "User installed apps" checkbox has gone from the installer and the
     GraphUserApps import flag is ignored if it is still present in a saved config.

     Why: the per-user log was written one row per user, per installed app, per metadata refresh. At a
     200,000-user tenant with ~20 apps per user that is roughly 4 million new rows a day - over a billion
     rows a year - for data that barely changes from one day to the next. In practice
     teams_addons_user_installed_log became the largest table in the database (bigger than audit_events,
     which backs most of the actual reporting) while feeding one rarely-used lookup category. It was also
     the slowest part of the Graph import and its biggest consumer of Graph request quota, because it
     required one Graph call per user per cycle.

   WHAT IT DOES
     Drops these three tables - but ONLY IF ALL THREE ARE EMPTY:
       * teams_addons                       - add-on / Teams app definitions
       * teams_addons_log                   - per-team, per-day add-on installs
       * teams_addons_user_installed_log    - per-user, per-day app installs (the huge one)

     IF ANY OF THEM CONTAINS EVEN ONE ROW, NOTHING IS DROPPED. All three tables, their indexes, their
     foreign keys and the reporting views over them are left exactly as they are, and the script prints a
     message telling you they were retained. Your historic data is safe, it simply stops being added to.

     When the tables ARE dropped (fresh installs, and tenants that never enabled the import) the script
     also tidies up what pointed at them:
       * drops the view vwTeamsAddOns_Log (it existed only to report add-ons);
       * rewrites the view vwTeamsStats without its AddOns CTE. The view keeps its exact column list,
         including "Add-Ons - Active Only", which becomes a constant 0 - so saved reports and queries
         that select that column keep working;
       * drops the foreign key teams_tabs.teams_addon_id -> teams_addons.

     NOT dropped in any case: the teams_tabs.teams_addon_id COLUMN itself. It is nullable and is simply no
     longer mapped by Entity Framework. Leaving it avoids rewriting teams_tabs, a table we are keeping,
     and EF ignores unmapped columns.

   RECLAIMING THE STORAGE (optional, admin's choice)
     If you keep the tables, nothing writes to them any more - they are read-only history. When you no
     longer need that history you can reclaim the space yourself, in your own maintenance window:

         ALTER TABLE dbo.teams_tabs DROP CONSTRAINT [FK_dbo.teams_tabs_dbo.teams_addons_teams_addon_id];
         DROP TABLE dbo.teams_addons_user_installed_log;
         DROP TABLE dbo.teams_addons_log;
         DROP TABLE dbo.teams_addons;
         DROP VIEW  dbo.vwTeamsAddOns_Log;   -- vwTeamsStats must then also be rewritten

     Easiest route: delete the rows (in batches, or TRUNCATE if there are no FK references you care
     about), then simply re-run this script - it will see the tables are empty, drop them and rewrite the
     views for you.

     DROP TABLE on a very large table is metadata-only for the table itself, but SQL Server still has to
     deallocate every extent; on a multi-hundred-GB table expect that to take minutes and to generate
     significant log activity. TRUNCATE TABLE is the cheaper way to empty it first.

   RUNTIME
     Effectively instant in every case:
       * the emptiness test is an EXISTS (not COUNT(*)), so it is O(1)-ish even on a billion-row table;
       * the drop path only ever runs against EMPTY tables;
       * there are no index rebuilds and no ALTER COLUMN, so nothing is rewritten and no ONLINE/OFFLINE
         edition gating is involved.
     No maintenance window is required for this migration, and the importer does not need to be stopped.

   SAFETY
     * Idempotent / re-runnable: everything is guarded, so an already-applied run is a no-op and the
       __MigrationHistory stamp is still reached on every path.
     * Non-destructive by design: no DELETE is issued anywhere, and a table that holds data is never
       dropped.
     * No wrapping transaction (matches suppressTransaction: true on the migration), so an interrupted
       run converges on re-run.

   PREREQUISITE
     The database must already be on migration 202608190622001_CopilotDroppedAuditFields (the previous
     release). This migration DOES change the EF entity model (three entities removed), so the stamp below
     embeds this migration's own model snapshot rather than copying the predecessor's row.
   ===================================================================================================== */

SET NOCOUNT ON;
GO


SET NOCOUNT ON;

DECLARE @migration nvarchar(100) = N'DeprecateTeamsAddons';
DECLARE @msg nvarchar(2000);
DECLARE @sql nvarchar(max);
DECLARE @hasRows bit;
DECLARE @anyRows bit = 0;
DECLARE @present int = 0;
DECLARE @estimate bigint;

DECLARE @defs sysname = N'teams_addons';
DECLARE @teamLog sysname = N'teams_addons_log';
DECLARE @userLog sysname = N'teams_addons_user_installed_log';

DECLARE @tables table (sequence int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, table_name sysname NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO @tables (sequence, table_name)
VALUES (1, N'teams_addons_user_installed_log'), (2, N'teams_addons_log'), (3, N'teams_addons');

DECLARE @i int = 1;
DECLARE @table sysname;

WHILE @i <= 3
BEGIN
    SELECT @table = table_name FROM @tables WHERE sequence = @i;

    IF OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.' + @table, N'U') IS NOT NULL
    BEGIN
        SET @present += 1;

        -- EXISTS rather than COUNT(*): teams_addons_user_installed_log can hold a billion rows and we
        -- only need to know whether it holds any. sys.partitions gives a cheap estimate for the log line.
        SET @hasRows = 0;
        SET @sql = N'SELECT @out = CASE WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM [dbo].[' + @table + N']) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END;';
        EXEC sp_executesql @sql, N'@out bit OUTPUT', @out = @hasRows OUTPUT;

        SELECT @estimate = ISNULL(SUM(rows), 0)
        FROM sys.partitions
        WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.' + @table) AND index_id IN (0, 1);

        IF @hasRows = 1
        BEGIN
            SET @anyRows = 1;
            SET @msg = @migration + N': dbo.' + @table + N' holds data (about '
                + CAST(@estimate AS nvarchar(20)) + N' rows).';
        END
        ELSE
            SET @msg = @migration + N': dbo.' + @table + N' is empty.';

        RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
    END

    SET @i += 1;
END

IF @present = 0
BEGIN
    SET @msg = @migration + N': no Teams add-on tables present; nothing to do.';
    RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
END
ELSE IF @anyRows = 1
BEGIN
    SET @msg = @migration + N': ***** Teams add-on tracking is DEPRECATED and the import has been removed. *****';
    RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
    SET @msg = @migration + N': your teams_addons / teams_addons_log / teams_addons_user_installed_log tables still contain data, so they have been LEFT IN PLACE and are now read-only historic data - nothing writes to them any more.';
    RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
    SET @msg = @migration + N': teams_addons_user_installed_log is typically the largest table in the database. To reclaim that storage, drop the tables by hand once you no longer need the history (drop the teams_tabs foreign key first), then re-run this migration script - it will confirm they are gone.';
    RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
END
ELSE
BEGIN
    SET @msg = @migration + N': all Teams add-on tables are empty; removing them.';
    RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;

    -- 1) Reporting views that read the add-on tables. vwTeamsAddOns_Log exists only to report add-ons,
    --    so it goes. vwTeamsStats keeps its shape (including the Add-Ons column, now a constant 0) so
    --    any saved report or query selecting those columns keeps working.
    IF OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.vwTeamsAddOns_Log', N'V') IS NOT NULL
    BEGIN
        EXEC sp_executesql N'DROP VIEW [dbo].[vwTeamsAddOns_Log];';
        RAISERROR('DeprecateTeamsAddons: dropped view vwTeamsAddOns_Log.', 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
    END

    IF OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.vwTeamsStats', N'V') IS NOT NULL
    BEGIN
        EXEC sp_executesql N'
ALTER VIEW [dbo].[vwTeamsStats] AS
    WITH
    Members AS (
        SELECT teams.name, COUNT(DISTINCT team_membership_log.user_id) AS [Member Count]
        FROM teams
        LEFT JOIN team_membership_log
            ON teams.id = team_membership_log.team_id
        GROUP BY teams.id, teams.name
    ),
    Channels AS (
        SELECT teams.name, COUNT(teams_channels.name) AS [Channel Count]
        FROM teams
        INNER JOIN teams_channels
            ON teams.id = teams_channels.team_id
        GROUP BY teams.name
    ),
    Tabs AS (
        SELECT teams.name, COUNT(DISTINCT teams_tabs.name) AS [Active Tab Count]
        FROM teams
        INNER JOIN teams_channels
            ON teams.id = teams_channels.team_id
        INNER JOIN teams_channel_tabs_log
            ON teams_channels.id = teams_channel_tabs_log.channel_id
        INNER JOIN teams_tabs
            ON teams_channel_tabs_log.tab_id = teams_tabs.id
        GROUP BY teams.name
    )
    SELECT Members.name AS [Team],
        COALESCE(Members.[Member Count], 0) AS [Members],
        COALESCE(Channels.[Channel Count], 0) AS [Channels],
        CAST(0 AS int) AS [Add-Ons - Active Only],
        COALESCE(Tabs.[Active Tab Count], 0) AS [Tabs - Active Only]
    FROM Members
    LEFT JOIN Channels
        ON Members.name = Channels.name
    LEFT JOIN Tabs
        ON Members.name = Tabs.name;';
        RAISERROR('DeprecateTeamsAddons: rewrote view vwTeamsStats without the add-on counts.', 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
    END

    -- 2) Foreign keys pointing AT the add-on tables from tables we are keeping (in practice just
    --    teams_tabs.teams_addon_id). Keys defined ON the dropped tables go with DROP TABLE.
    DECLARE @fkName sysname, @fkTable nvarchar(300);
    DECLARE fk_cursor CURSOR LOCAL FAST_FORWARD FOR
        SELECT fk.name, QUOTENAME(SCHEMA_NAME(t.schema_id)) + N'.' + QUOTENAME(t.name)
        FROM sys.foreign_keys AS fk
        INNER JOIN sys.tables AS t ON t.object_id = fk.parent_object_id
        WHERE fk.referenced_object_id IN (
                OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.teams_addons'),
                OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.teams_addons_log'),
                OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.teams_addons_user_installed_log'))
          AND fk.parent_object_id NOT IN (
                OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.teams_addons'),
                OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.teams_addons_log'),
                OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.teams_addons_user_installed_log'));

    OPEN fk_cursor;
    FETCH NEXT FROM fk_cursor INTO @fkName, @fkTable;
    WHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0
    BEGIN
        SET @sql = N'ALTER TABLE ' + @fkTable + N' DROP CONSTRAINT ' + QUOTENAME(@fkName) + N';';
        EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
        SET @msg = @migration + N': dropped foreign key ' + @fkName + N' on ' + @fkTable + N'.';
        RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;

        FETCH NEXT FROM fk_cursor INTO @fkName, @fkTable;
    END
    CLOSE fk_cursor;
    DEALLOCATE fk_cursor;

    -- 3) The tables themselves, children first.
    SET @i = 1;
    WHILE @i <= 3
    BEGIN
        SELECT @table = table_name FROM @tables WHERE sequence = @i;

        IF OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.' + @table, N'U') IS NOT NULL
        BEGIN
            SET @sql = N'DROP TABLE [dbo].[' + @table + N'];';
            EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
            SET @msg = @migration + N': dropped table dbo.' + @table + N'.';
            RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
        END

        SET @i += 1;
    END

    SET @msg = @migration + N': Teams add-on tracking removed. Note dbo.teams_tabs.teams_addon_id is left in place (nullable, unused) so the teams_tabs table does not need rewriting.';
    RAISERROR(@msg, 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
END
GO

/* =====================================================================================================
   RECORD THE MIGRATION IN __MigrationHistory
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   EF decides what is outstanding by comparing the migrations compiled into Common.Entities.dll with the
   rows in dbo.__MigrationHistory. Without this stamp the installer / DatabaseUpgrader would try to apply
   DeprecateTeamsAddons again on the next upgrade, and the Health page would report it as pending.

   This migration changes the EF entity model (TeamAddOnDefinition, TeamAddOnLog and UserAppsLog are
   removed, as is TeamTabDefinition.TeamAddOnDefinition), so the Model column cannot be copied from the
   predecessor row. The snapshot below is the gzipped EDMX from
   202608191533567_DeprecateTeamsAddons.resx, emitted in hex chunks.
   ===================================================================================================== */

IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dbo.__MigrationHistory WHERE MigrationId = N'202608191533567_DeprecateTeamsAddons')
BEGIN
    IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dbo.__MigrationHistory WHERE MigrationId = N'202608190622001_CopilotDroppedAuditFields')
    BEGIN
        DECLARE @model varbinary(max);
SET @model = 
SET @model = @model + 
SET @model = @model + 
SET @model = @model + 
SET @model = @model + 
SET @model = @model + 
SET @model = @model + 
SET @model = @model + 
SET @model = @model + 
SET @model = @model + 

        INSERT INTO dbo.__MigrationHistory (MigrationId, ContextKey, Model, ProductVersion)
        SELECT N'202608191533567_DeprecateTeamsAddons', ContextKey, @model, ProductVersion
        FROM dbo.__MigrationHistory
        WHERE MigrationId = N'202608190622001_CopilotDroppedAuditFields';
        RAISERROR('DeprecateTeamsAddons: recorded in __MigrationHistory.', 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
    END
    ELSE
        RAISERROR('DeprecateTeamsAddons: the schema change ran, but prerequisite migration 202608190622001_CopilotDroppedAuditFields is missing from __MigrationHistory, so this migration was not stamped.', 16, 1);
END
ELSE
    RAISERROR('DeprecateTeamsAddons: already recorded in __MigrationHistory.', 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT;
GO